From 83d0d2fae845a20d52e7b5c74346254c81aa9b76 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: StartE <yili0618@qq.com>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 15:37:42 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] fix(sight): report pids in the observer namespace

Every consumer of an event pid resolves it against user-space's own /proc
-- cmdline, exe, maps, cgroup, and the /proc/<pid>/root paths uprobes
attach to -- but the BPF side reported the target's innermost
pid-namespace pid. The two agree only when observer and target share a
namespace, which is why agentsight on a host and a sidecar with
shareProcessNamespace both worked and the distinction went unnoticed. A
DaemonSet with hostPID:true breaks it: the innermost pid is a
container-local number that on the host either does not exist or belongs
to an unrelated process, so discovery matched that process's cmdline
instead, failed, and silently attached nothing -- not even a warning,
because the wrong /proc entry reads back fine.

Add a const volatile observer_pidns_is_init rodata flag, derived from the
inode of /proc/self/ns/pid, and report the host tgid when it is set.
Neither pid flavour is unconditionally correct, so the observer's location
has to reach BPF somehow, and user-space cannot recover it afterwards:
reversing a namespace pid means scanning every /proc/*/status for an NSpid
match, which is O(processes) per exec event and ambiguous because the same
container-local pid can exist in several containers.
bpf_get_ns_current_pid_tgid() does not help either -- it returns -EINVAL
unless the namespace named by (dev, ino) is the current task's own, so it
translates nothing into an ancestor namespace. Carrying the answer in
rodata keeps the branch constant-folded away by the verifier at load time,
and defaulting it to false leaves an unconfigured object bit-for-bit on
the previous behaviour.

The flag is deliberately narrow rather than a general namespace-chain
resolver. An observer that itself lives in a namespace and watches a
target in a deeper nested one still gets the innermost pid, exactly as
before; fixing that needs a bounded walk over the target's upid chain,
which is more verifier surface than the two supported deployments justify.
AgentScanner's empty-cmdline check keeps that case fail-closed, so it
declines to attach rather than probing the wrong process.

Fixes: b6a95632 ("feat(sight): support container PID namespace in BPF traced
 process filtering and event emission")
Assisted-by: Claude Code:2.1.211
